    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change is coherently additive and follows the existing conventions of this codebase.\n\n- **Mapper helper placement.** `telephoneDisplay(Owner)` mirrors the existing `displayName`/`initials`/`membershipNumber` helpers exactly \u2014 same `@Mapping(... expression = \"java(...)\")` wiring, same null-guard shape, same delegation to a domain helper. A maintainer reading `OwnerMapper` finds nothing surprising.\n\n- **Formatting logic lives in the right unit.** The country-code knowledge (`NATIONAL_DIGITS_BY_COUNTRY_CODE`, E.164 parsing via `toE164`) already lives in `TelephoneNormalizer`. Putting `toDisplay` there reuses that data rather than duplicating the country-code table into a new formatter class, which is the better trade-off. The mapper stays thin and delegates.\n\n- **The refactor is a genuine deduplication, not a copy-paste.** Extracting `matchLongestCountryCode` out of `hasValidNationalLength` is the right move: both the existing length check and the new display method need \"find the longest matching country code,\" and the extraction gives each a single named helper with a clear Javadoc. `hasValidNationalLength` keeps its own concern (length validation) and now reads more directly. No logic was duplicated to add the feature.\n\n- **Nothing is becoming a catch-all.** The additions are proportionate: one private helper, one public presentation method, one mapper delegate. The OpenAPI schema addition sits alongside the sibling read-only computed fields with matching structure.\n\nThe one arguable point is that a class named `*Normalizer` in a `validation` package is now also doing presentation formatting. But given the phone-number data and parsing already live there, keeping the display logic co-located is more natural than spawning a parallel class that would re-declare the same country-code knowledge, so this is the right call for this codebase.",
